From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 19 Apr 1994 10:54:24 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-137] Simanarchy

T. David Burns writes:
>Simulation versus formal analysis - I think these are separate issues, and
>we'll be lucky to be able to combine them. A good sim type game should be
>fun to play, very detailed and realistic. Such a game would be very
>difficult to analyze. (I think your average MOO would be absolutely
>intractable.) A good game theory approach should try to abstract from the
>details, concentrating on the essential strategic interactions in a simple
>(and probably boring to play) way.

Yes, the more detail, the more fun the game (up to a point), but also
the more work to make the game.  Anyway, you'd want to be sure
somewhere in all that detail the game actually contained real anarchy,
instead of say contracts enforced by a benevolent government.  You
might ensure this by making a minimal anarchy game, and then building
all the detail on top of that.  This has the advantage of sharing work
with efforts to do game models and experiments.

>Axelrod's work can be interpreted as supporting the feasibility of
>decentralized/anarchic/PPL type systems (under certain conditions).

Well this is very weak support at best.  Slightly better evidence is a
paper by a prof of mine here (Ordeshook) analysing a game model
showing you can have stable anarchy between nations.  There are likely
even better models out there - I haven't done a lit survey.  And if we
put our minds to it, we should be able to do even better.

Nancy suggests:
>The game may need some sort of production by the Sims--this would
>plausibly both increase the rewards of cooperation and give the
>cooperators more leverage to hire protection-- or would it mostly
>increase the temptation to attack them?

Well they definitely need things worth having, stealing, and losing,
and rewards from cooperation.  Sure, this could be from production.

Robin Hanson

